Z||000000||V6pack||01-14-2000||08:38 PM||||Looking through previous threads I did not find much on other OEM wheels that would fit Fieros. <P>I read on the Fiero Online Service Guide that the 5 lug 100mm Chrysler Lebaron 16X6's would fit a Fiero. So far so good, but I don't know/can't find the offset for those wheels.<P>I have heard about the Cavilier, Grand Am and others but I found some unused Lebaron's for cheap on eBay that would look just fine on my GT. They are even white to match my monochrome paint scheme.  Seller doesn't respond.<P>Does anyone know, remember, or have 1st hand experience with these wheels?  Are the lug pockets cones or round?<P>The FSOG is a pretty good resource, but I'm reluctant to go ahead because I don't know how to measure the offset to make sure.  Any ideas? opinions?<BR>As always,  I appreciate the help.<BR>Thanks<BR>V6pack <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-14-2000||11:38 PM||||nearly all U.S. OEM wheels use cone faced lugs nuts. GM, Chrysler, and Ford do at the least. (that is on cars made here. All 3 import some cars.) Actually finding the spec should be easy for the chrysler wheel. Any "friendly" chrysler dealer should have it in their service books or in the options list.<P>In your case a simple backset measure will tell you if they are close. Pretty much all 6 inch wide wheels that bolt to Fiero need the same backset.<P>Simple backset: the measurement from a straight edge across the rear flang to the mounting surface of the wheel.<P>Off set: Simple backset minus flange thickness minus 1/2 wheel width.<P>A simple example: If the simple backset is 4 inches and the flange is 1/2 inch thick and the wheel is 6 inches wide. ((You have to eliminate the flange thickness. wheel width is the distance to the bead seats inside the flanges.))<P>4-.5=3.5"<P>1/2 the width is 3".<P>3.5-3=+.5 offset<P>That means the wheel center is moved in .5" from it's mounting face. A negative, also called reverse offset, would move it out.<P>Wider wheels need less offset or even negative offset to keep the same simple backset. If you increase simple backset very much you will likely hit something with the wheel.<P>To make this more complicated, you are increasing the rim diameter. This can some times allow more simple backset, but only sometimes. Most times you have to keep the mounted tire diameter and backset close to OEM. ((Mounted tire backset is not the same as wheel backset. the tire will be slightly wider than the rim most of the time.)) If the rolling diameter gets way off you mess up the speedo and ECM, but that should only be an issue if your putting on monster truck tires. A larger diameter tire will also reach inboard more when turning and could scrub. More than an inch or 2 change and you may have to limit the steering to prevent it. This won't hurt regular driving, but it will make parking a bit more interesting.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||01-15-2000||01:43 PM||||I almost bought a set of those wheels. I seem to remember that the backspacing is identical to the backspacing of the 14x6 Fiero wheel. The reason that I didn't buy was because I wanted something 7" or wider. They would have looked nice, tho'. ||||reg||1
